.TH XUDO-RULES 5 "2026-11-13" "xudo 0.1.0-beta.1" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
xudo-rules \- rule files for xudo
.SH DESCRIPTION
A rule file allowlists named actions.
It does not allowlist a command string.
Each action names one absolute executable and one typed argument list.
The caller's tokens fill only positions the action declared.
.PP
The file is TOML.
Unknown keys are a hard error, on the file and on every table in it
.RB ( deny_unknown_fields ).
A key that is silently ignored would be a constraint the operator believes
is in force.
A file that fails to parse fails the whole directory.
.SH LOCATION
The default directory is
.IR /etc/xudo/rules.d/ .
Only names that end in
.B .toml
and have something before that suffix are rule files.
A file named
.B .toml
is not one.
Names and contents must be UTF-8.
.PP
Files are loaded in byte order of their names, whatever order the directory
returns them.
Duplicate action ids fail the load.
.PP
The broker and the gate load the directory through a trust walk.
Every component from the filesystem root must be owned by uid 0, must not be
group- or world-writable, and must not sit on a mount a non-root user could
have made.
The front-end reads the same directory by path, so
.B xudo explain
works on a checkout.
It does not execute.
.PP
Provenance is assigned by the loader.
Each action's source is the file name and the line of its
.B id
declaration.
A rule cannot set
.BR source :
that key is rejected.
.SH FILE
The top-level keys are
.BR action ,
written as one
.B [[action]]
table per action, and
.BR test ,
written as one
.B [[test]]
table per test (see
.BR TESTS ).
.TP
.B id
Stable identifier, for example
.BR xudo.svc.reset\-failed .
.TP
.B tier
One of
.BR diag\-read ,
.BR svc\-control ,
.BR pkg\-install ,
.BR pkg\-remove ,
.BR sys\-tunable ,
.BR net\-filter ,
.BR critical .
.B critical
always demands confirmation.
.TP
.B exe
Absolute path of the program.
A relative path, an empty component,
.BR . ,
or
.B ..
is rejected.
.B PATH
is never searched.
.TP
.B args
The argument list, position by position.
Omitted or empty means zero arguments and nothing else.
Each element is a table with
.BR name ,
.BR matcher ,
and optionally
.BR repeat .
.B name
is cited in traces.
Two positions in one action may not share a name.
.TP
.B confirm
When a human must approve.
.B default
(the default) follows
.BR confirm_agent_actions ,
which starts true, so an enrolled agent is confirmed.
.B always
confirms for every caller.
.B never
asks for no confirmation from this action.
It is not a grant against a stricter overlapping action, and a
.B critical
action cannot set it.
Overlapping actions resolve to the strictest requirement.
File order does not decide whether confirmation is demanded.
.TP
.B agent\-allowed
Whether an enrolled agent may run this action.
The default is true.
.B false
makes it human-only.
.TP
.B env\-allow
Environment variable names to pass through, by exact name.
The default is none.
The child's environment is built from scratch, so a name on no list does not
exist for the child.
.SH TESTS
A rule file may carry
.B [[test]]
tables that state what the rules are expected to decide.
.B xudo check
runs them, and so does the build for the shipped files.
.TP
.B argv
Array of strings: the program, then its operands, compared byte for byte.
A program with no operands is a one-element array; an empty array is a test
error.
.TP
.B expect
.BR allow ,
.BR deny ,
or
.BR confirm .
.B confirm
means permitted once a human approves.
.TP
.B action
Optional.
The action that must decide the command, as
.B xudo explain \-\-action
would.
Without it every action that runs the program takes part.
An id the ruleset does not define is a test error, not a pass.
.TP
.B reason
Optional text shown when the test fails.
Displayed with terminal escapes removed.
.PP
A test is always decided for a caller classified as a human, with the default
settings, so the result does not depend on the machine.
Agent behaviour cannot be expressed in a test:
.B agent\-allowed = false
still passes
.BR "expect = allow" ,
and a confirmation that only an agent would be asked for is reported as
.BR allow .
A test that
.B expect
s a
.B deny
and names an
.B action
is also put to the whole ruleset, as
.B xudo explain
would, and fails when that permits the command.
When
.B xudo check
finds a failing test and a lint error in one run, it exits with the test
failure status, which ranks above the lint error, and the JSON report carries
both.
Keys are kebab-case and an unknown key is a hard parse failure, like every
other table.
A test can never change a decision: it is parsed beside the ruleset, not into
it, and it is not part of the ruleset generation, so adding or editing a test
does not make a request minted under the same rules stale.
A test cannot set its own provenance; the loader records the file and the line
of its header.
.SH MATCHERS
Matcher keys are kebab-case.
A position accepts one of them.
.TP
.B literal
Exactly these bytes.
.TP
.B one\-of
Exactly one of these byte strings.
.TP
.B int\-range
A base-10 integer in the inclusive range
.BR lo .. hi .
No leading
.BR + ,
no surrounding space, no underscores, and no leading zero on a multi-digit
number.
.TP
.B path\-under
An absolute path lexically equal to
.B prefix
or beneath it.
A relative path, a
.B ..
component, or an embedded NUL is refused, and
.B /etc/nginx\-evil
is not under
.BR /etc/nginx .
This is a syntactic pre-filter.
It does not see symlinks, bind mounts, or hardlinks.
Containment at execution is a separate check.
.TP
.B name
An identifier class:
.BR deb\-name ,
.BR rpm\-name ,
.BR arch\-name ,
.BR apk\-name ,
.BR unit\-name ,
.BR service\-unit\-name ,
.BR hostname ,
.BR sysctl\-key .
Every class rejects an empty value and a value longer than 255 bytes.
A leading
.BR \- ,
or any occurrence of
.BR / ,
.BR = ,
.BR : ,
or
.B ..
anywhere in the value is rejected.
.B unit\-name
must end in
.BR .service ,
.BR .socket ,
.BR .timer ,
.BR .target ,
.BR .path ,
or
.BR .mount ,
and may contain
.B @
and a backslash.
A suffix-less name would match more units than it appears to, because
.B systemctl restart foo
resolves
.B foo
to
.BR foo.service .
The journal rules use this kind: reading any unit's log is the point.
.B service\-unit\-name
accepts only a name ending in
.BR .service ,
with no
.B @
and no backslash.
.BR poweroff.target ,
.BR modprobe@dummy.service ,
and an escaped name are not a service restart.
The shipped lifecycle and reset\-failed rules use this kind.
.B unit\-name
itself is not tightened.
.TP
.B pattern
A regular expression.
Anchors are added by the parser; a pattern that already starts with
.B ^
or ends with
.B $
is rejected.
The compiled pattern is size-bounded.
.SH REPEAT
.B repeat
is how many arguments one position consumes.
.B one
(the default) is exactly one.
.B optional
is zero or one.
.B between
takes
.B min
and
.BR max ,
inclusive, and
.B max
is clamped to 64.
.SH ARGUMENTS AS DELIVERED
A rule matches the argv byte for byte as the kernel delivers it.
Canonicalization drops a trailing
.B \-\-
that separates nothing, and does nothing else.
It does not split
.BR \-\-key=value .
A value can itself look like a flag, and splitting had no fixed point.
.PP
A rule that accepts both spellings lists both.
.B \-\-lines=20
is one argument;
.B \-\-lines
and
.B 20
are two.
The engine will not turn one spelling into the other.
Prefer the joined spelling where one token is enough, because that is what
people type.
.SH DENY\-LIST
After an action matches, a deny-list compiled into the binary is evaluated
on the canonical argv.
No rule file can disable, reorder, shadow, or override it.
It is enumerated by capability class (a shell, an interpreter, an arbitrary
read or write, a unit-definition change, and the rest of that list), not by
binary name.
A copy, a hardlink, or a multicall applet does not escape a class.
On
.B systemctl
the list also refuses the unit, whatever the verb: a power or boot target,
a
.B modprobe@
template, an fsck, growfs, makefs, or
.B mdadm@
template, an
.B xudod
or
.B xudo\-
unit, and
.B apparmor
or
.BR auditd .
A rule that still uses
.B unit\-name
cannot start those.
.SH EXAMPLE
From the shipped
.IR 20\-services.toml .
The verb is an enumeration, and the unit is a plain service name.
.PP
.EX
[[action]]
id = "xudo.svc.reset\-failed"
tier = "svc\-control"
exe = "/usr/bin/systemctl"
args = [
  { name = "no\-pager", matcher = { literal = "\-\-no\-pager" } },
  { name = "verb", matcher = { literal = "reset\-failed" } },
  { name = "unit", matcher = { name = "service\-unit\-name" } },
]
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xudo (1)
